\providecommand{\codel}[2]{\texttt{#1\allowbreak#2}}

\section{A parser that fails is not a parser that explains}

Every parser fails eventually.  A user typing a configuration file will
put a bracket in the wrong place; a program feeding a query language will
send a token the grammar does not accept.  At that moment the parser
hands back a \code{ParseError}, and the quality of the answer the user
gets is entirely determined by two things: how far into the input the
error points, and how accurately it describes what would have been
acceptable there.

DSLtk's parser core provides the first of these two things and half of
the second.  The error record carries a byte offset, and the merge rule
of Chapter~\ref{ch:parser-core} ensures that the offset is the farthest
one any alternative reached.  The error record also carries a list of
labels, and the merge rule ensures that alternatives which failed at the
same offset contribute their labels to a single list.  What the library
does \emph{not} provide is the last step: turning a position and a list
of labels into a sentence.  There is no formatter in the header, no
\code{format_error}, no \code{to_string} on \code{ParseError}, and no
notion of a line or a column.  All of that is the user's job, and this
chapter is largely about doing that job well.

The chapter also covers the two facilities that surround the error
record: \code{run_parser}, the entry point that runs a parser over a
whole string and deals with trailing input, and \code{parse_with_action},
the hook that lets a grammar hand matched text to a semantic action.
Finally it covers the small registry of compile-time-keyed production
handlers, which is the shipped mechanism for dispatching to a semantic
action by production identity.

\section{A worked example}

Listing~\ref{lst:ch23-diagnostics} is a complete program.  It defines a
tiny grammar --- an assignment of the form \code{name = number} --- and
three things built on top of it: a message formatter, a line-and-column
calculator, and a demonstration of the semantic-action hook and of the
production registry.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={A diagnostic formatter, a semantic action, and a production-handler registry, built on a tiny assignment grammar.},label={lst:ch23-diagnostics}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"

#include <cctype>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <vector>

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1.  The message formatter.  The header stores a ParseError; turning it
//     into a sentence is the user's job.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

static std::string
format_expected (const std::vector<std::string> &labels)
{
  if (labels.empty ())
    return "<nothing>";
  std::string s;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < labels.size (); ++i)
    {
      if (i)
        {
          if (i + 1 == labels.size ())
            s += (labels.size () == 2) ? " or " : ", or ";
          else
            s += ", ";
        }
      s += labels[i];
    }
  return s;
}

// ParseError carries a byte offset.  Turning that offset into a line and
// a column is also the user's job.
static std::string
format_error (const std::string &src, const dsl::ParseError &err)
{
  std::size_t line = 1;
  std::size_t column = 1;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < err.pos && i < src.size (); ++i)
    {
      if (src[i] == '\n')
        {
          ++line;
          column = 1;
        }
      else
        ++column;
    }
  return "line " + std::to_string (line) + ", column "
         + std::to_string (column) + " (offset " + std::to_string (err.pos)
         + "): expected " + format_expected (err.expected);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2.  A tiny grammar:   name = number
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// lexeme (p) -- match p, then swallow the whitespace that follows.
template <typename T>
static dsl::Parser<T>
lexeme (dsl::Parser<T> p)
{
  return dsl::parser (
      [p] (dsl::ParsecInput &in) -> dsl::ExpectedResult<T>
      {
        auto r = p (in);
        if (!r)
          return r;
        while (!in.eof ()
               && std::isspace (static_cast<unsigned char> (in.peek ())))
          in.consume ();
        return *r;
      });
}

static dsl::Parser<char>
digit ()
{
  return dsl::satisfy (
      [] (char c)
      { return std::isdigit (static_cast<unsigned char> (c)) != 0; },
      "digit");
}

static dsl::Parser<char>
letter ()
{
  return dsl::satisfy (
      [] (char c)
      { return std::isalpha (static_cast<unsigned char> (c)) != 0; },
      "letter");
}

static dsl::Parser<char>
alnum ()
{
  return dsl::satisfy (
      [] (char c)
      { return std::isalnum (static_cast<unsigned char> (c)) != 0; },
      "alphanumeric character");
}

static dsl::Parser<std::string>
name ()
{
  auto core = dsl::operator& (letter (), dsl::operator* (alnum ()));
  return lexeme (dsl::parser (
      [core] (dsl::ParsecInput &in) -> dsl::ExpectedResult<std::string>
      {
        auto r = core (in);
        if (!r)
          return dsl::ExpectedResult<std::string>::failure (
              r.error.pos, r.error.kind, r.error.expected);
        std::string s (1, (*r).first);
        for (char c : (*r).second)
          s += c;
        return s;
      }));
}

static dsl::Parser<std::string>
digits ()
{
  auto core = dsl::operator& (digit (), dsl::operator* (digit ()));
  return lexeme (dsl::parser (
      [core] (dsl::ParsecInput &in) -> dsl::ExpectedResult<std::string>
      {
        auto r = core (in);
        if (!r)
          return dsl::ExpectedResult<std::string>::failure (
              r.error.pos, r.error.kind, r.error.expected);
        std::string s (1, (*r).first);
        for (char c : (*r).second)
          s += c;
        return s;
      }));
}

// assignment := name '=' digits
static dsl::Parser<std::pair<std::string, std::string>>
assignment ()
{
  auto core = dsl::operator& (
      dsl::operator& (name (), lexeme (dsl::ch ('='))), digits ());
  return dsl::parser (
      [core] (dsl::ParsecInput &in)
          -> dsl::ExpectedResult<std::pair<std::string, std::string>>
      {
        auto r = core (in);
        if (!r)
          return dsl::ExpectedResult<std::pair<std::string, std::string>>::
              failure (r.error.pos, r.error.kind, r.error.expected);
        return std::make_pair ((*r).first.first, (*r).second);
      });
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3.  Semantic actions and the production registry.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

using SemMap = std::unordered_map<std::string, std::string>;
using Handler = std::function<std::string (const SemMap &, dsl::ParserStage)>;

template <dsl::FixedString Id, typename Fn>
static void
register_handler (std::unordered_map<std::string, Handler> &table,
                  dsl::ProductionHandler<Id, Fn> handler)
{
  table[std::string (Id.view ())] = [handler] (const SemMap &sem,
                                               dsl::ParserStage stage)
  { return handler.fn (sem, stage); };
}

static std::unordered_map<std::string, Handler>
build_handlers ()
{
  std::unordered_map<std::string, Handler> table;
  register_handler (
      table,
      dsl::production<"name"> (
          [] (const SemMap &, dsl::ParserStage stage)
          { return "name production matched \"" + std::string (stage.span)
                  + "\""; }));
  register_handler (
      table,
      dsl::production<"value"> (
          [] (const SemMap &, dsl::ParserStage stage)
          { return "value production matched \"" + std::string (stage.span)
                  + "\""; }));
  return table;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4.  main
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

static void
show (const std::string &src)
{
  auto outcome = dsl::run_parser (assignment (), src);
  std::cout << "  input: \"" << src << "\"\n";
  if (outcome.value)
    std::cout << "    ok: " << outcome.value->first << " = "
              << outcome.value->second << "\n";
  else
    std::cout << "    " << format_error (src, outcome.error) << "\n";
}

int
main ()
{
  std::cout << "--- run_parser ---\n";
  show ("width = 12");
  show ("width = ");
  show ("width 12");
  show ("width = 12 trailing");
  show ("= 12");

  std::cout << "\n--- parse_with_action ---\n";
  {
    dsl::ParsecInput in{ "width = 12", 0 };
    SemMap semantics;
    auto out = dsl::parse_with_action (
        assignment (), in, semantics,
        [] (const SemMap &sem, dsl::ParserStage stage)
        {
          return "action saw span=\"" + std::string (stage.span)
                 + "\" begin=" + std::to_string (stage.begin)
                 + " end=" + std::to_string (stage.end)
                 + " semantics=" + std::to_string (sem.size ());
        });
    std::cout << "  " << (out ? *out : std::string ("<no action>")) << "\n";
  }

  std::cout << "\n--- production registry ---\n";
  {
    auto table = build_handlers ();
    dsl::ParsecInput in{ "width = 12", 0 };
    SemMap semantics;
    semantics["name"] = "width";
    auto stage = dsl::parse_with_action (
        assignment (), in, semantics,
        [] (const SemMap &, dsl::ParserStage s) { return s; });
    if (stage)
      {
        auto it = table.find ("name");
        if (it != table.end ())
          std::cout << "  " << it->second (semantics, *stage) << "\n";
      }
  }
  return 0;
}
\end{lstlisting}

The output is:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:ch23-diagnostics}.},label={lst:ch23-diagnostics-out}]
--- run_parser ---
  input: "width = 12"
    ok: width = 12
  input: "width = "
    line 1, column 9 (offset 8): expected digit
  input: "width 12"
    line 1, column 7 (offset 6): expected =
  input: "width = 12 trailing"
    line 1, column 12 (offset 11): expected <end-of-input>
  input: "= 12"
    line 1, column 1 (offset 0): expected letter

--- parse_with_action ---
  action saw span="width = 12" begin=0 end=10 semantics=0

--- production registry ---
  name production matched "width = 12"
\end{lstlisting}

Four of those five diagnostics are good.  The fourth is interesting: the
input \code{"width = 12 trailing"} parses successfully as far as the
grammar is concerned, and the error comes from \code{run_parser} itself,
which refuses to leave text behind.  The fifth is the mirror image of
the second: the error is at offset zero, because the name rule failed
immediately, and the digit rule was never reached.

\section{The data model}

The diagnostic facilities are five entities: a result type, a runner, a
stage record, an action hook, and a handler registry.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{The diagnostic and semantic-action facilities of the parser library.}
\label{tab:ch23-helpers}
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{5pt}
\begin{tabular}{L{2.6cm} L{5.8cm} L{4.8cm}}
\toprule
\textbf{Entity} & \textbf{Signature} & \textbf{Behaviour} \\
\midrule
\codel{ParseOutcome}{<T>}
  & \code{std::optional<T> value}, \code{ParseError error}
  & The result of running a whole parser over a whole input.  It is
    \emph{not} a variant: both members are always present.  A successful
    outcome carries a default-constructed error. \\
\code{run_parser}
  & \code{template <typename T> ParseOutcome<T> run_parser (const Parser<T>& p, std::string_view source)}
  & Runs \code{p} on a fresh \code{ParsecInput}.  On failure it returns
    the parser's error verbatim.  On success, if the input has not been
    fully consumed it synthesises a \code{Committed} error labelled
    \code{"<end-of-input>"} at the current offset. \\
\code{ParserStage}
  & \code{std::size_t begin}, \code{std::size_t end},
    \code{std::string_view span}
  & The extent of a successful match: the offsets it began and ended at,
    and the text between them. \\
\code{parse_with_action}
  & \code{template <typename T, typename ActionFn> auto parse_with_action (const Parser<T>& p, ParsecInput& input, const std::unordered_map<std::string, std::string>& semantics, ActionFn&& action)}
  & Runs \code{p} on \code{input}, builds a \code{ParserStage} from the
    consumed span, and calls \code{action(semantics, stage)}.  Returns an
    empty \code{std::optional} if the parse failed; the error is
    discarded. \\
\code{production}
  & \code{template <FixedString Id, typename Fn> auto production (Fn&& fn)}
  & Builds a \code{ProductionHandler<Id, Fn>} whose single member
    \code{fn} is the supplied callable.  The identifier is a compile-time
    string and appears only in the \emph{type}. \\
\codel{Production}{Handler<Id, Fn>}
  & \code{Fn fn}
  & A registry entry.  It holds the handler and nothing else --- in
    particular it does \emph{not} expose the identifier, so a registry
    keyed at run time must be given the key separately. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Two of these deserve a closer reading.

\code{run_parser} is the only function in the library that owns the
whole-input convention, and it is worth spelling out exactly what it
does, because the behaviour is not obvious from the name.  It creates a
\code{ParsecInput} over the given view, runs the parser, and then:

\begin{enumerate}
\item If the parser failed, the error is returned \emph{unchanged} ---
    same position, same kind, same labels.  \code{run_parser} does not
    improve, filter or annotate it.
\item If the parser succeeded but the cursor is not at the end of the
    input, a new error is synthesised: offset equal to the cursor's
    position, kind \code{Committed}, and a single label
    \code{"<end-of-input>"}.  The value is discarded.
\item Otherwise the value is returned together with a default-constructed
    \code{ParseError}.
\end{enumerate}

The consequence is that a successful parse of a prefix is a
\emph{failure} as far as \code{run_parser} is concerned, and the
message says so in a way that is often exactly right --- but not always.
``expected \code{<end-of-input>}'' is a good message for a program that
read too much, and a poor one for a program that meant to parse only the
first record of a file.  The library offers no way to ask
\code{run_parser} to stop early; a caller that wants a prefix parse must
call the parser directly and inspect the cursor.

\code{parse_with_action} is the semantic-action hook, and its shape
deserves attention.  It takes four arguments, not two: the parser, an
\emph{existing} \code{ParsecInput}, a map of semantics, and the action.
The input is passed in rather than created, so a caller may run several
parsers over one input and accumulate results; the map of semantics is
passed in for the same reason, and is the channel through which
sub-parsers hand their results to one another.  The action is called
with the map and a \code{ParserStage}, and its return value is what the
function returns, wrapped in a \code{std::optional}.  The return type is
computed from a call of the action with the semantics map and a
default-constructed stage, so the
action must be callable with those two arguments and must have a
deducible return type.

Note what \code{parse_with_action} does \emph{not} do.  It does not
check that the input has been consumed, and it does not report an error:
if the parser fails, the function returns an empty \code{std::optional}
and the \code{ParseError} is thrown away.  A caller that wants the error
must run the parser itself, or call \code{run_parser} and then invoke
the action separately.

\section{From a ParseError to a sentence}

The header stops at the error record.  Everything a user reads is written
by the user, and the job has three parts: find the position, decide
which labels to show, and put them in a grammatical order.

The position part is a scan.  Because \code{ParseError::pos} is a byte
offset, the line and the column are found by walking the source from the
beginning and counting newlines; the column is the number of bytes since
the last newline, not the number of characters, which is a distinction
that matters for any input that is not pure ASCII.  The function
\code{format_error} in Listing~\ref{lst:ch23-diagnostics} does exactly
this, and the cost is linear in the length of the source.  A tool that
parses a file of a megabyte should cache the line starts, or should not
re-scan for every error.

The label part is where the design decision of
Chapter~\ref{ch:parser-core} becomes visible.  The \code{expected} list
is a \emph{set} of labels that failed at the same offset, and the
natural rendering is a disjunction: ``expected X'', ``expected X or Y'',
``expected X, Y, or Z''.  The formatting has to decide where to put the
Oxford comma, and Listing~\ref{lst:ch23-diagnostics} uses the rule that
two labels are joined with ``or'' and three or more with ``, or''.

\begin{pseudocode}[Format an expected set]
\textbf{function} format\_expected (labels)\\
\quad if labels is empty then\\
\qquad return "<nothing>"\\
\quad s := ""\\
\quad n := size (labels)\\
\quad for i from 0 to n - 1 do\\
\qquad if i > 0 then\\
\qquad\quad if i = n - 1 then\\
\qquad\qquad\quad if n = 2 then\\
\qquad\qquad\qquad\quad s := s + " or "\\
\qquad\qquad\quad else\\
\qquad\qquad\qquad\quad s := s + ", or "\\
\qquad\quad else\\
\qquad\qquad\quad s := s + ", "\\
\qquad s := s + labels[i]\\
\quad return s
\end{pseudocode}

The grammatical order of the list is itself a decision, and it is one the
library has already made, accidentally but consistently.  As
Chapter~\ref{ch:parser-core} explains, \code{operator|} merges the
\emph{second} alternative's error as the destination, so the first
alternative's labels are appended to the second's.  A grammar that tries
\code{digit} first and \code{'('} second therefore reports ``expected
\code{(} or \code{digit}''.  The set is right; the order is the reverse
of the source order.  A formatter that wants the source order must sort
the labels, and a grammar that wants a particular order must control the
order of the alternatives, because there is no mechanism in the library
for reordering a label set.

\section{Failure versus diagnostic}

The two words in the title of this section are not synonyms, and the
distinction is the most useful thing in this chapter.

A \emph{failure} is a control-flow signal.  It says: this alternative
does not apply, and here is a position and a set of labels that a user
might find useful \emph{if} no alternative applies.  A failure is
consumed by the machinery: it is merged, it is swallowed by
\code{optional}, it is demoted by \code{try_parse}, it is replaced by
\code{labeled}.  A failure that is never consumed --- that is, a failure
that reaches the top of the parse --- becomes a diagnostic.

A \emph{diagnostic} is a message for a human.  It wants the furthest
position that any alternative reached, because that is the point at which
the parser had the most context and therefore the most to say.  It wants
the union of the labels of every alternative that failed there, because
each of them is a thing the user could have written.  And it wants a
sentence, not a data structure.

The library optimises the first two of those and leaves the third to the
user.  The consequence is that the quality of a diagnostic is a property
of the \emph{grammar}, not of the library.  A grammar in which every
alternative is plausible at the point of failure produces a good
diagnostic; a grammar in which one alternative is a catch-all produces a
diagnostic that lists the catch-all's label alongside the meaningful
ones, and the message becomes worse, not better, as the grammar grows.

The classic example is the \code{factor} rule of
Chapter~\ref{ch:primitive-parsers}: a factor is a number or a
parenthesised expression.  At the point where a factor is required, the
parser has two perfectly good reasons to fail, and the union of their
labels is ``expected \code{(} or \code{digit}''.  But a user who has
typed \code{1 +} is not going to be helped by being told that a left
parenthesis would have been acceptable.  The rule is right, the message
is noise, and the only cure is at the grammar level: the author must
decide which alternatives are worth advertising, and must use
\code{labeled} to say so.

There is a second reason why the union can mislead.  The merge rule
unions the labels of alternatives that failed at the \emph{same} offset,
but it does not check that they failed for the same \emph{reason}.  Two
alternatives can fail at the same offset with completely different
expectations, and the union then says ``expected X or Y'' when the truth
is ``expected X, and if not X then Y''.  For a language with a small
number of alternatives this is a cosmetic problem; for a language with
dozens, the message becomes a list of the vocabulary of the language
rather than a diagnosis.

\begin{warnbox}[The union grows with the grammar]
Every alternative that can fail at a given offset contributes a label to
the union at that offset.  A grammar with a hundred productions therefore
produces messages with up to a hundred labels, and the user is left to
find the relevant one.  The standard remedy is to wrap the individual
rules in \code{labeled} with a short description of the construct, so
that the union contains a dozen short phrases rather than a hundred
single characters.
\end{warnbox}

\subsection{The merge lattice}

Figure~\ref{fig:ch23-lattice} draws the merge operation as a lattice:
two error records are combined into one, and the two possible shapes of
the result --- replacement and union --- are shown side by side.

\begin{dslgraph}
