    {\texttt{e1} : pos 5, soft, \{\texttt{digit}\}};
  \node[dslbox, text width=34mm] (e2) at (0.2,1.6)
    {\texttt{e2} : pos 7, soft, \{\texttt{identifier}\}};
  \node[dslbox, text width=30mm, fill=decisionbg] (m1) at (-1.6,0.2)
    {merge};
  \node[dslbox, text width=40mm, fill=termbg] (r1) at (-1.6,-1.1)
    {pos 7, soft, \{\texttt{identifier}\} \quad (the farther error wins)};

  \node[dslbox, text width=34mm] (e3) at (3.4,1.6)
    {\texttt{e3} : pos 5, soft, \{\texttt{digit}\}};
  \node[dslbox, text width=34mm] (e4) at (6.8,1.6)
    {\texttt{e4} : pos 5, committed, \{\texttt{keyword}\}};
  \node[dslbox, text width=30mm, fill=decisionbg] (m2) at (5.1,0.2) {merge};
  \node[dslbox, text width=40mm, fill=termbg] (r2) at (5.1,-1.1)
    {pos 5, committed, \{\texttt{digit}, \texttt{keyword}\}};

  \draw[dslledge] (e1) -- (m1);
  \draw[dslledge] (e2) -- (m1);
  \draw[dslledge] (m1) -- (r1);
  \draw[dslledge] (e3) -- (m2);
  \draw[dslledge] (e4) -- (m2);
  \draw[dslledge] (m2) -- (r2);
\end{dslgraph}
\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{The two shapes of \code{merge_error}.  On the left the source
error is farther along and replaces the destination wholesale.  On the
right the two errors are at the same offset, so their label lists are
unioned and the kind is promoted to \code{Committed}.}
\label{fig:ch23-lattice}
\end{figure}

The promotion to \code{Committed} on a tie is easy to overlook and it has
a purpose.  Without the promotion, a union could be discarded by a later
merge: if a third error arrives at the same offset with a single label,
it would be merged into the union, and the union would survive --- but if
the third error is \emph{farther} along, the whole union would be
replaced by that error, losing the two labels that had been collected.
Promoting the union to \code{Committed} does not by itself prevent that
replacement, but it does make the union's status visible in the record,
and it makes the tie case distinguishable from the ordinary soft-failure
case in any diagnostic the user chooses to write.

\section{Semantic actions}

\code{parse_with_action} is the shipped mechanism for doing something
with a match beyond turning it into a value.  It has three moving parts.

The \code{ParserStage} records \emph{where} the match happened.  Its
\code{begin} and \code{end} members are the offsets before and after the
parser ran, and its \code{span} member is the text between them, taken
from the input with \code{get_span(begin)}.  The span is a
\code{std::string_view} into the source, so it is valid exactly as long
as the source is; a semantic action that wants to keep the matched text
must copy it.

The \code{semantics} map is the channel between rules.  It is a
\code{std::unordered_map<std::string, std::string>} from a production
identifier to the text that production matched, and it is passed to the
action so that the action can consult what the sub-rules found.  The map
is passed \emph{by const reference}, so the action reads it and the
caller owns it; an action that wants to add to it needs a non-const
reference of its own.

The action itself is an ordinary callable.  It receives the map and the
stage and returns whatever the caller wants; the return type is deduced
from a call of the action with the map and a default-constructed stage,
so the action must be callable with those two arguments and must have a
return type that can be deduced from that call.

The demonstration in Listing~\ref{lst:ch23-diagnostics} shows the shape
of the interaction: the action prints the span, the offsets and the size
of the semantics map, and the map is empty because the grammar of the
example has no rules that publish to it.

\section{Production handlers}

\codel{production<}{Id>(fn)} and \codel{ProductionHandler<}{Id, Fn>} are a very
small idea.  A \codel{Production}{Handler} holds one callable and nothing
else; the identifier is a compile-time \code{FixedString} that appears
only in the \emph{type} of the handler.  The purpose is to let a grammar
dispatch to a handler by production identity: the identity is known at
compile time, so a table of handlers can be built once, and the
dispatch is a lookup by string at run time.

The catch is that \code{ProductionHandler} does not expose the
identifier.  There is no \code{id} member, no \code{key()} method and no
\code{operator string}; the identifier lives in the template parameter
and nowhere else.  A run-time registry therefore has to be given the key
by hand, and the natural way to do that is a small template function
that reads it out of the type:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Registering a production handler under a run-time key derived from its compile-time identifier.},label={lst:ch23-register}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"

#include <functional>
#include <string>
#include <unordered_map>

using SemMap = std::unordered_map<std::string, std::string>;
using Handler = std::function<std::string (const SemMap &, dsl::ParserStage)>;

template <dsl::FixedString Id, typename Fn>
static void
register_handler (std::unordered_map<std::string, Handler> &table,
                  dsl::ProductionHandler<Id, Fn> handler)
{
  table[std::string (Id.view ())] = [handler] (const SemMap &sem,
                                               dsl::ParserStage stage)
  { return handler.fn (sem, stage); };
}
\end{lstlisting}

\code{FixedString::view()} is a \code{constexpr} member that returns the
characters of the identifier without the terminating null, so the key
that ends up in the map is exactly the string that appeared in the
template argument.  The cost of the scheme is one \code{std::string}
allocation per registered handler, paid once at start-up.

\section{Edge cases and limits}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{There is no message formatter in the header.}  No
  \code{to_string}, no \code{format}, no \code{operator<<} for
  \code{ParseError} or \code{ParseOutcome}.  Everything a user reads is
  written by the user, and the quality of the message is a property of
  the grammar as much as of the formatter.

\item \textbf{There is no line or column in the error record.}  Only a
  byte offset.  The conversion is a linear scan unless the caller caches
  the line starts.

\item \textbf{The offset is a byte offset, not a character offset.}  A
  multi-byte UTF-8 character counts as several columns.  A formatter that
  wants to report a column in characters must decode.

\item \textbf{\code{run_parser} discards the value of a trailing-input
  failure.}  The \code{ParseOutcome} it returns has an empty value, even
  though the parser itself succeeded and produced one.  A caller that
  wants the value anyway must call the parser directly.

\item \textbf{\code{run_parser} always requires the whole input.}  There
  is no flag for parsing a prefix, and no way to parse a stream of
  records.  A caller who wants that must drive the parser and the cursor
  by hand.

\item \textbf{\code{parse_with_action} discards the error.}  A failed
  parse yields an empty \code{std::optional} and nothing else; the
  \code{ParseError} is lost.  Combining the action hook with a
  diagnostic therefore requires running the parser twice, or running it
  once by hand.

\item \textbf{\code{parse_with_action} does not check for trailing
  input.}  Unlike \code{run_parser}, it will happily succeed on a
  prefix.  This is a feature --- it is what makes it possible to parse
  one record at a time --- but it means the caller must decide whether
  the rest of the input is acceptable.

\item \textbf{The stage's span is a view.}  \code{ParserStage::span} is
  a \code{std::string_view} into the source, valid only as long as the
  source.  A semantic action that stores it in a longer-lived structure
  must copy it.

\item \textbf{\code{production} does not register anything.}  It builds a
  value.  There is no global registry, no \code{register_production}
  function and no lookup; the user builds whatever table is wanted, and
  the identifier is not available at run time from the handler alone.

\item \textbf{The semantics map is stringly typed.}  A production
  identifier is a string, and a semantic action that reads the map gets
  a string; a typo in a key is a runtime failure, not a compile-time
  one.  The compile-time keying of the handler is the only place where
  the library offers static checking.

\item \textbf{The label list is never deduplicated within one error.}
  The de-duplication in \code{merge_error} only happens when two errors
  are merged; a parser that records the same label twice keeps both.

\item \textbf{There is no recovery.}  Nothing in the library resynchronises
  the input after an error, finds the next plausible token, or reports
  more than one error.  A diagnostic is a single position with a single
  set of labels.
\end{itemize}

\section{Closing}

The parser library stops at the error record, and that is a deliberate
choice rather than an omission.  The library computes what it is good at
--- the furthest position, the union of the labels that were plausible
there --- and leaves the presentation to the user, who knows what the
input was, where it came from, and what the user should be told.

The facilities around the error record fill in the rest of the pipeline.
\code{run_parser} is the entry point that decides what ``the whole
input'' means.  \code{parse_with_action} is the hook that connects a
match to a computation, and \code{ParserStage} is the record of where
the match happened.  \code{production} and \code{ProductionHandler} are
the dispatch mechanism that lets a grammar route a match to the code
that knows what to do with it.

The next chapter leaves parsing behind and looks at the other small
machinery in the header: the task pipeline, whose tasks communicate
through a shared, stringly typed map.
}
